CHANGE 7 — Trigger handling: ignore triggers pointing at a displayed modal
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
            var trigger = e.target.closest("." + className);
            if (trigger != null) {
                var formId = trigger.getAttribute(formIdAttribute);
                var form = document.getElementById(formId);
                if (form != null) {
                    if(trigger.getAttribute(modalWidthsAttribute) != null){
                        modalWidths = trigger.getAttribute(modalWidthsAttribute).split(",");
                    }else{
                        modalWidths = defaultModalWidths;
                    }
                    show(form);
                }
            }

NEW_SECTION
        document.addEventListener("click", function(e) {
            var trigger = e.target.closest("." + className);
            if (trigger == null) return;

            var modal = document.getElementById(trigger.getAttribute(formIdAttribute));
            if (modal == null) return;
            if (layerOf(modal) != null) return; //resolved to the displayed copy of an already open modal

            if (modalWidthsAttribute != "" && trigger.getAttribute(modalWidthsAttribute) != null) {
                modalWidths = trigger.getAttribute(modalWidthsAttribute).split(",");
            } else {
                modalWidths = defaultModalWidths;
            }
            show(modal);
        }, false);

REASON
Since the displayed copy carries the modal's id while the source gives it up,
getElementById() during an open modal returns the copy inside an overlay. A trigger
pointing at an already displayed modal would therefore hand that copy to show() and
the module would try to open a node that is itself part of a layer. The layerOf()
check rejects it.

The modalWidthsAttribute guard is also new: when the property is unset,
getAttribute("") was being called on every trigger click.

================================================================================
CHANGE 8 — Idempotent open/close completion (fixes duplicate openProcessor)
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
(no equivalent — the old transitionend handler ran its completion logic every time
it matched, and relied on the modalOn flag being false to ignore repeats)

NEW_SECTION
function completeOpen(layer) {
    if (layer.opened) return; //the box effect transitions two properties, so transitionend fires twice
    layer.opened = true;

    layer.host.innerHTML = layer.sourceOuter;
    layer.host.style["height"] = layer.height + "px";

    var content = layer.host.childNodes[0];
    if (content != null) {
        content.style["display"] = "block";
        content.style["width"] = "100%";
    }
    //the animation boxes are kept, hidden, because the closing effect replays through them
    if (layer.effectsCon != null) layer.effectsCon.style["display"] = "none";
    layer.openProcessor();
}

function completeClose(layer) {
    if (layer.destroyed) return;
    layer.destroyed = true;
    ...
}

REASON
Measured against the previous build: the box effect fired openProcessor twice on
every open (verified by counting callback invocations per effect — none/split/flip
reported 1, box reported 2). Its animation box uses `transition: all`, which animates
both width and height, so transitionend fires once per property and the completion
logic ran twice. Consumers using openProcessor to focus a field or fetch data were
doing it twice.

The equivalent double fire on close was masked rather than fixed: releaseModal() ran,
set modalOn = false, and the handler's `if (modalOn == true)` guard swallowed the
second event. That guard disappears with the stack, so the idempotence has to be
explicit. Both completion functions are now guarded by per-layer flags, and closing
is additionally guarded at entry by layer.closing so a second Escape during a close
animation cannot start the teardown twice.

