NEW_SECTION
    none: function(layer) {
        layer.overlay.classList.add("show");
        completeOpen(layer);
    },

//Copies the modal into an animation box. The copy drops its id so that only the
//one finally handed to the user (in the layer host) carries it.
function fillEffectBox(box, layer) {
    box.innerHTML = layer.sourceOuter;
    var copy = box.childNodes[0];
    copy.removeAttribute("id");
    copy.style["display"] = "block";
    return copy;
}

function startCloseTransition(layer, boxSelector) {
    layer.effectsCon.classList.remove("trans_in");
    layer.effectsCon.classList.add("trans_out");
    layer.effectsCon.style["display"] = "block";
    layer.host.style["display"] = "none";
    return layer.effectsCon.querySelector(boxSelector);
}

REASON
All eight effect functions took the modal element and reached for module state and
global ids; they now take a layer and touch only that layer's nodes. The bookkeeping
each of them repeated — creating the host, tagging and blanking the source, storing
the outerHTML, showing the overlay — moved into openLayer() and completeOpen(), so
the effect functions contain only their animation.

Three blocks that were duplicated four times each are extracted: fillEffectBox()
(copy the modal into an animation box, strip the copy's id, reveal it) and
startCloseTransition() (swap trans_in for trans_out, reveal the effects container,
hide the host, return the box to animate). The internal ids eleft/eright/flipper/
flpform/fBG/Boxform become classes vLeft/vRight/vFlipper/vFlipForm/vFlipBg/vBoxForm,
all carrying the shared marker class vEffectBox that the transitionend handler keys
on. They are transient nodes removed with their overlay, so nothing external
referenced them.

A typo is corrected in passing: the flipper's inline CSS declared
"backface-visibilty" (missing an 'i'), which no browser applied. It now reads
backface-visibility, so the flip effect hides its reverse face as intended.

================================================================================
CHANGE 10 — Responsive widths applied on open, for every effect
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    function fixModalWidth(mainModal){
        var sbpoint = new ScreenBreakPoint(brkpoints);
        if (sbpoint.screen.mode == "large") {
            mainModal.style["width"] = modalWidths[0];
        } else if (sbpoint.screen.mode == "medium") {
            mainModal.style["width"] = modalWidths[1];
        } else if (sbpoint.screen.mode == "small") {
            mainModal.style["width"] = modalWidths[2];
        }
    }

NEW_SECTION
function applyWidths(layer) {
    var mode = new ScreenBreakPoint(layer.brkpoints).screen.mode;
    if (mode == "large") {
        layer.space.style["width"] = layer.widths[0];
    } else if (mode == "medium") {
        layer.space.style["width"] = layer.widths[1];
    } else {
        layer.space.style["width"] = layer.widths[2];
    }
}

REASON
Beyond taking a layer, this fixes a gap and carries one deliberate behaviour change.

fixModalWidth() was only ever called from the transitionend handler and the resize
handler. The "none" effect reaches neither, so with the default effect the responsive
widths were never applied on open — config.modalWidths / modalWidthsAttribute /
screenBreakPoints simply had no effect until the user happened to resize the window.
The "none" effect compensated by sizing its host from the modal's own computed width.

applyWidths() is now called from openLayer() for every effect, so the documented
responsive width behaviour holds uniformly.

BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, called out explicitly: a modal opened with effect "none" was
previously sized to its own content width and is now sized by the breakpoint triple
(default 500px / 500px / 86%). Consumers relying on content-width sizing with the
default effect will see a different width and should set config.modalWidths, or the
per-trigger widths attribute, to match their previous size. This was judged a fix
rather than a regression because the widths configuration is documented as applying
to the modal regardless of effect, and because the previous behaviour made three
config properties silently inert for the default effect. The trailing branch is also
changed from `else if (mode == "small")` to `else` so that no breakpoint mode can
leave the width unset.

================================================================================
CHANGE 11 — Blur released by reference check rather than unconditionally
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        if (overlayBackgroundType == "blur" && pageContainer != null) pageContainer.classList.remove("vxKit");

NEW_SECTION
function releaseBlur(layer) {
    if (layer.overlayBackgroundType != "blur" || layer.pageContainer == null) return;
    for (var x = 0; x < modalStack.length; x++) { //a layer still up may want the same container blurred
        if (modalStack[x].overlayBackgroundType == "blur" && modalStack[x].pageContainer === layer.pageContainer) return;
    }
    layer.pageContainer.classList.remove("vxKit");
}

REASON
With stacking, closing one layer must not unblur a page that a layer still on screen
wants blurred. The removal is now conditional on no remaining layer targeting the
same container, which makes the blur behave like a reference count without needing
one. The single-modal case is unaffected: with an empty stack the loop finds nothing
and the class is removed as before.

